course: dictionary
lesson: 8039
title: Reggae: a music style from Jamaica
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reggae is a kind of popular music that began in Jamaica, an island in the Caribbean Sea. It has a strong off-beat rhythm, so the beat falls between the main beats, and you can often feel it in your feet.
explain: Reggae songs often sing about love, peace, faith and fairness. Use the context to work out the meaning: «music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa.» Jazz and samba are kinds of music, so reggae must be one too.
explain: Reggae can also describe things, as in «a reggae band» or «a reggae beat». Ask: what word would fit here? Try it: a jazz band fits, so reggae is telling us what kind of band it is.
fact: Reggae is a noun for a style of music. It is uncountable, so you say «I love reggae», not «I love a reggae».
fact: Reggae grew in Jamaica in the late 1960s. Bob Marley is its most famous singer.
fact: When reggae describes something, it is an adjective, as in reggae band, reggae beat or reggae music.
fact: Reggae grew from mixing African rhythms with other kinds of music, just as jazz and blues did.
fact: You can hear reggae at carnivals, such as the Notting Hill Carnival in London.
exercise: Where does reggae come from? | Scotland | Kenya | Canada | *Jamaica, an island in the Caribbean
exercise: «music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa.» What is reggae in this sentence? | A type of food | A dance step | *A kind of music | A country
exercise: In «a reggae band», what does reggae tell us? | The name of the band | How many bands there are | Where the band is playing | *What kind of band it is
test: Which singer is famous for reggae music? | Elvis Presley | Mozart | Michael Jackson | *Bob Marley
test: Which ideas are often sung about in reggae songs? | Cooking and baking | Speed and racing | *Peace and fairness | Snow and ice
exam: Which sentence uses reggae as the name of a music style? | *We danced to reggae at the carnival. | We ran to the reggae in the park. | The reggae was a bright red bus.
